---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import tools from '../../data/tools.json';

const tool = tools.find(t => t.slug === 'freelance-project-scope-estimator')!;
---

<ToolLayout tool={tool}>

  <div slot="tool">
    <h2 style="font-family:'Sora',sans-serif; font-size:1.1rem; font-weight:700; color:var(--text-primary); margin-bottom:1.25rem;">Freelance Project Scope Estimator</h2>

    <div style="margin-bottom:1rem;">
      <label style="font-size:0.78rem; font-weight:600; color:var(--text-secondary); text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:0.05em; display:block; margin-bottom:0.4rem;">Your Hourly Rate ($)</label>
      <input id="hourly-rate" type="number" value="100" min="1" step="5"
        class="input-themed"
        style="width:200px;"/>
    </div>

    <div id="scope-list" style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:0.5rem; margin-bottom:1.25rem;"></div>

    <button id="add-item" class="btn-secondary" style="width:100%; padding:0.625rem; border-style:dashed;">+ Add Scope Item</button>

    <div style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,#4F46E5,#6366F1); border-radius:0.875rem; padding:1.25rem; color:#fff; margin-top:1.25rem; text-align:center;">
      <div style="font-size:0.78rem; font-weight:600; text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:0.08em; opacity:0.85; margin-bottom:0.25rem;">Total Estimate Range</div>
      <div style="font-family:'Sora',sans-serif; font-size:2.25rem; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:-0.02em;">
        $<span id="out-low">0</span> – $<span id="out-high">0</span>
      </div>
      <div style="font-size:0.82rem; opacity:0.75; margin-top:0.35rem;"><span id="out-hours">0</span> hrs estimated</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div slot="how-to-use">
    <h2 class="font-display seo-h2">How to Use the Freelance Project Scope Estimator</h2>
    <p class="seo-p">Freelancers frequently lose money by underquoting projects or lose clients by overestimating compared to competitors. The root cause is guessing at a single number instead of building a structured estimate grounded in actual deliverables. A scope estimator solves this by breaking your project into phases, assigning hour ranges to each, and producing a budget range that accounts for both best-case and worst-case scenarios. With that range in hand, you can quote clients with confidence and protect your margins from the start.</p>
    <div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:0.625rem; margin-bottom:0.75rem">
      <div class="border-theme surface-2 seo-card-sm">
        <strong style="color:var(--text-primary); font-size:0.875rem">Map Out Deliverables First</strong>
        <p style="font-size:0.85rem; line-height:1.65; margin:0.35rem 0 0">Before touching any numbers, list every deliverable your project requires. A freelance web designer building a five-page marketing site for a SaaS startup might include discovery, wireframes, high-fidelity mockups, responsive front-end build, basic CMS setup, and two rounds of revisions. Turning each of these into a distinct scope item ensures nothing falls through the cracks and gives you a clear picture of the full engagement.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-theme surface-2 seo-card-sm">
        <strong style="color:var(--text-primary); font-size:0.875rem">Calibrate Hours to Your Experience</strong>
        <p style="font-size:0.85rem; line-height:1.65; margin:0.35rem 0 0">Set a minimum and maximum hour range for every scope item based on how long similar work has taken you in the past. A mobile app developer estimating a food-delivery MVP might assign 12 to 20 hours for user authentication, 30 to 50 hours for the order flow, and 8 to 14 hours for push notifications. The spread between low and high captures uncertainty without inflating the quote artificially.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-theme surface-2 seo-card-sm">
        <strong style="color:var(--text-primary); font-size:0.875rem">Layer in Non-Labor Costs</strong>
        <p style="font-size:0.85rem; line-height:1.65; margin:0.35rem 0 0">Projects rarely involve only your time. A content strategist managing a product launch might add $200 for stock photography, $150 for a premium analytics tool, and $300 for a copywriting subcontractor. A full-stack developer might include $50 per month for staging hosting and $29 per month for a third-party API. Adding these as custom line items ensures your estimate covers the true cost of delivery.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="border-theme surface-2 seo-card-sm">
        <strong style="color:var(--text-primary); font-size:0.875rem">Compare the Range to Your Gut</strong>
        <p style="font-size:0.85rem; line-height:1.65; margin:0.35rem 0 0">After building the estimate, review the total range against your intuition. If a project shows a $6,000 to $9,500 range and you were expecting closer to $12,000, you may have under-scoped a deliverable or set hours too low for your experience level. Adjust individual items until the range aligns with your professional judgment, then present the low-to-high figure to the client so expectations stay realistic.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div slot="how-it-works">
    <h2 class="font-display seo-h2">How the Freelance Project Scope Estimator Works</h2>
    <p class="seo-p">The estimator calculates a cost range by multiplying your hourly rate against the sum of minimum and maximum hours across all active scope items. Every calculation happens locally, so sensitive rate and project information never leaves your device.</p>
    <h3 class="font-display seo-h3-sm">Scope-Based Cost Model</h3>
    <p class="seo-p">Each project type comes with a set of <code class="inline-code">scope items</code> representing typical deliverables such as discovery, design, development, and testing. You assign a <code class="inline-code">minimum hours</code> and <code class="inline-code">maximum hours</code> estimate to every active item. The low total is the sum of all enabled minimums multiplied by your rate; the high total is the sum of all maximums. This produces a realistic budget range rather than a single fragile number.</p>
    <p class="seo-p">For example, a designer billing $120 per hour might enable six items: Discovery at 6 to 10 hours, Wireframes at 10 to 16, Visual Design at 16 to 24, Frontend Build at 20 to 32, QA at 6 to 10, and Revisions at 4 to 8. The minimum totals 62 hours ($7,440) and the maximum totals 100 hours ($12,000), giving the designer a defensible $7,440 to $12,000 range to present to the client.</p>
    <h3 class="font-display seo-h3-sm">Expense and Overhead Calculation</h3>
    <p class="seo-p">Beyond hourly labor, the estimator lets you add custom items for fixed costs such as <code class="inline-code">hosting</code>, <code class="inline-code">software licenses</code>, <code class="inline-code">travel</code>, and <code class="inline-code">third-party services</code>. Each custom item contributes directly to the total, ensuring your estimate captures the full cost of delivery, not just your personal time.</p>
    <p class="seo-p">A full-stack developer might add a $200 monthly staging server, a $49 per month API subscription, and a one-time $350 stock photography purchase. These three items add $799 to the estimate regardless of hours worked, preventing the common mistake of absorbing project expenses out of pocket.</p>
    <h3 class="font-display seo-h3-sm">Margin and Contingency Analysis</h3>
    <p class="seo-p">The difference between the low and high estimates creates a natural <code class="inline-code">contingency buffer</code>. This spread covers scope refinements, client revisions, and unexpected issues without requiring a separate markup. When presenting to clients, the range lets you anchor the conversation around a fair midpoint while preserving room for profit.</p>
    <p class="seo-p">If your estimate ranges from $8,000 to $12,500, the $4,500 spread acts as built-in insurance. You can quote the midpoint around $10,250 and treat the upper bound as a reserve for unplanned work. When the project stays on track, the difference between actual hours and the high estimate becomes additional margin without renegotiation.</p>
  </div>

  <div slot="faq">
    <h2 class="font-display seo-h2-faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="seo-faq-list" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage">
      <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" class="surface-2 border-theme seo-faq-card">
        <h3 itemprop="name" class="seo-faq-q">Should I quote fixed price or hourly?</h3>
        <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
          <p itemprop="text" class="seo-faq-a">Fixed-price projects give clients budget certainty and let you earn more when you work efficiently. Hourly billing suits ongoing or poorly defined work where scope changes frequently. Many freelancers combine the two, charging a fixed price for discovery and design, hourly for development, and a monthly retainer for maintenance. A good rule of thumb: if you can define at least 80 percent of the deliverables upfront, fixed price usually works in your favor.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" class="surface-2 border-theme seo-faq-card">
        <h3 itemprop="name" class="seo-faq-q">How do I handle scope creep?</h3>
        <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
          <p itemprop="text" class="seo-faq-a">Prevent scope creep by including a detailed scope document with your quote that lists what is excluded and how many revision rounds are covered. Use a formal change order process for any additions requested mid-project, specifying the cost and timeline impact of each change before beginning work. The earlier you set these boundaries, the less friction you will face during execution. Document every agreed change in writing, including the cost impact, so both parties have a clear record if disputes arise later.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" class="surface-2 border-theme seo-faq-card">
        <h3 itemprop="name" class="seo-faq-q">What contingency should I add to estimates?</h3>
        <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
          <p itemprop="text" class="seo-faq-a">A common approach is to build a 20 to 30 percent buffer into your estimate to cover client communication overhead, unexpected technical issues, and revision rounds. Less experienced freelancers may want to start at 40 to 50 percent until they have calibrated their estimation accuracy against real project outcomes. Track your actual hours against estimates on every project so you can tighten the range over time and stop leaving money on the table. Over a series of completed projects, this data becomes your most valuable estimation asset.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" class="surface-2 border-theme seo-faq-card">
        <h3 itemprop="name" class="seo-faq-q">How should I present this estimate to a client?</h3>
        <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
          <p itemprop="text" class="seo-faq-a">Present a range such as $4,500 to $6,000 rather than a single figure, paired with a clear breakdown by deliverable. Explain that the range depends on final scope decisions and revision rounds, and identify the assumptions behind each estimate so the client understands what drives the cost. This sets honest expectations and makes the lower end feel like a win for the client. If the client pushes back on price, you can identify which deliverables to defer or simplify rather than cutting your rate across the board.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" class="surface-2 border-theme seo-faq-card">
        <h3 itemprop="name" class="seo-faq-q">How do I estimate hours for a project I have not done before?</h3>
        <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
          <p itemprop="text" class="seo-faq-a">Break the project into smaller, familiar tasks and estimate each one individually, then add them together for a total. Research similar projects on freelance platforms or speak with peers who have built comparable products to ground your numbers in reality. Start with your best guess, then add a higher contingency buffer until you have real data from your own completed projects. Consider whether the unfamiliar technology or domain is worth the risk at your current rate, or whether a smaller paid discovery phase would reduce uncertainty before committing to a full quote.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" class="surface-2 border-theme seo-faq-card">
        <h3 itemprop="name" class="seo-faq-q">Should I include contingency in my freelance quote?</h3>
        <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
          <p itemprop="text" class="seo-faq-a">Yes. Every freelance project carries uncertainty, and contingency protects both you and the client from surprise costs. Build it into your rate or hour range rather than listing it as a separate line item so the quote remains clean and professional. When scope stays within bounds, the contingency becomes additional profit; when it does not, it absorbs the overage without requiring renegotiation. Clients appreciate receiving a complete number rather than seeing a line item that suggests you expect things to go wrong.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" class="surface-2 border-theme seo-faq-card">
        <h3 itemprop="name" class="seo-faq-q">How does this compare to using a spreadsheet or project management tool?</h3>
        <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
          <p itemprop="text" class="seo-faq-a">Spreadsheets require you to build formulas from scratch and are easy to break with accidental edits, especially when sharing with clients or team members. Project management platforms track tasks after the work begins but rarely help with the initial quoting phase where most estimation errors originate. A scope estimator is purpose-built for the pre-sale stage, producing a client-ready budget range in minutes rather than hours. You can still export the numbers into a proposal or invoice template after the estimate is finalized.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" class="surface-2 border-theme seo-faq-card">
        <h3 itemprop="name" class="seo-faq-q">What if the project involves multiple developers or subcontractors?</h3>
        <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
          <p itemprop="text" class="seo-faq-a">Add each contributor as a separate line item with their own hourly rate and hour range. A lead developer at $150 per hour and a junior developer at $75 per hour should not share the same rate field. Account for coordination overhead, code review time, and handoff meetings, which typically add 10 to 15 percent to a multi-person project compared to solo work. This gives you an accurate blended total instead of a misleading average that could leave you short on the actual invoice.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" class="surface-2 border-theme seo-faq-card">
        <h3 itemprop="name" class="seo-faq-q">Why is a range more useful than a single-number estimate?</h3>
        <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
          <p itemprop="text" class="seo-faq-a">A single number implies false precision and leaves no room for the uncertainty inherent in creative and technical work. A range acknowledges that requirements evolve, client feedback takes time, and unforeseen technical challenges arise. It also gives you negotiating flexibility, allowing you to offer a lower price for a reduced scope without appearing to discount your work. Clients generally respond better to transparency about variability than to a figure that later needs revision.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" class="surface-2 border-theme seo-faq-card">
        <h3 itemprop="name" class="seo-faq-q">What happens when the estimate does not match the final invoice?</h3>
        <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
          <p itemprop="text" class="seo-faq-a">Some variance is normal, and a well-built range should absorb minor overages without issue. If actual hours consistently fall outside the high end of your estimate, review which scope items were under-scoped and adjust those ranges for future projects. If the gap is significant, discuss it with the client early and reference the original scope document to determine whether the additional work constitutes a change order. Tracking estimates against invoices on every project builds a feedback loop that sharpens your accuracy over time.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" class="surface-2 border-theme seo-faq-card">
        <h3 itemprop="name" class="seo-faq-q">How do I adjust the estimate if the client wants to cut the budget?</h3>
        <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
          <p itemprop="text" class="seo-faq-a">Rather than lowering your hourly rate, reduce the scope by disabling or trimming specific line items. Show the client which deliverables will be removed or simplified, and let them decide what to prioritize. This preserves your margins while giving the client control over what gets built. If the budget reduction is small, consider absorbing it by tightening the hour range on lower-risk items rather than cutting scope across the board.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" class="surface-2 border-theme seo-faq-card">
        <h3 itemprop="name" class="seo-faq-q">Should I use this estimate for recurring or retainer projects?</h3>
        <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
          <p itemprop="text" class="seo-faq-a">Retainer work benefits from estimation because it grounds the monthly fee in actual deliverables rather than gut feeling. Build an estimate for one month of work, then negotiate a retainer fee at or near the midpoint of the range. Revisit the estimate quarterly as the scope of the retainer evolves and new deliverables are added. This prevents the common problem of retainer work gradually expanding beyond the original agreement without a corresponding rate adjustment.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="seo-disclaimer" style="margin-top:1.5rem; background:var(--bg-surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); font-size:0.8rem; color:var(--text-muted); line-height:1.7; padding:1rem 1.25rem; border-radius:0.5rem">
      <strong style="display:block; margin-bottom:0.35rem">Disclaimer</strong>
      This tool provides budget estimates for project planning only. Actual project costs depend on scope changes, contractor rates, local market conditions, and unforeseen issues. Always obtain professional quotes.
    </div>
  </div>

</ToolLayout>

<script>
  interface ScopeItem { id: number; label: string; min: number; max: number; enabled: boolean; }

  let nextId = 0;
  let items: ScopeItem[] = [
    { id: nextId++, label: 'Discovery & requirements',      min: 4,  max: 8,  enabled: true },
    { id: nextId++, label: 'UI / UX design (wireframes)',   min: 8,  max: 16, enabled: true },
    { id: nextId++, label: 'Frontend development',          min: 16, max: 40, enabled: true },
    { id: nextId++, label: 'Backend / API development',     min: 12, max: 30, enabled: false },
    { id: nextId++, label: 'Database design & migrations',  min: 4,  max: 10, enabled: false },
    { id: nextId++, label: 'QA & testing',                  min: 6,  max: 12, enabled: true },
    { id: nextId++, label: 'DevOps / deployment setup',     min: 4,  max: 10, enabled: false },
    { id: nextId++, label: 'Third-party integrations',      min: 6,  max: 16, enabled: false },
    { id: nextId++, label: 'Client revisions (2 rounds)',   min: 4,  max: 8,  enabled: true },
    { id: nextId++, label: 'Project management & comms',    min: 4,  max: 8,  enabled: true },
  ];

  function getRate(): number {
    return parseFloat((document.getElementById('hourly-rate') as HTMLInputElement)?.value) || 100;
  }

  function updateTotals() {
    const rate   = getRate();
    const active = items.filter(i => i.enabled);
    const minHrs = active.reduce((s, i) => s + i.min, 0);
    const maxHrs = active.reduce((s, i) => s + i.max, 0);
    (document.getElementById('out-low') as HTMLElement).textContent   = (minHrs * rate).toLocaleString();
    (document.getElementById('out-high') as HTMLElement).textContent  = (maxHrs * rate).toLocaleString();
    (document.getElementById('out-hours') as HTMLElement).textContent = minHrs === maxHrs ? String(minHrs) : `${minHrs}–${maxHrs}`;
  }

  function renderList() {
    const list = document.getElementById('scope-list')!;
    list.innerHTML = '';
    items.forEach(item => {
      const row = document.createElement('div');
      row.className = 'result-card';
      row.style.cssText = 'display:flex;align-items:center;gap:0.5rem;padding:0.5rem 0.75rem;';
      row.innerHTML = `
        <input type="checkbox" ${item.enabled ? 'checked' : ''} data-id="${item.id}" style="accent-color:var(--color-brand-blue);width:16px;height:16px;cursor:pointer;flex-shrink:0;"/>
        <input type="text" value="${item.label}" data-id="${item.id}" data-field="label"
          style="flex:1;border:none;outline:none;font-size:0.85rem;color:var(--text-primary);background:transparent;min-width:0;"/>
        <input type="number" value="${item.min}" min="0" data-id="${item.id}" data-field="min"
          style="width:52px;padding:0.2rem 0.4rem;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:0.35rem;font-size:0.78rem;text-align:center;color:var(--text-secondary);outline:none;background:var(--bg-surface);"/>
        <span style="font-size:0.72rem;color:var(--text-muted);">–</span>
        <input type="number" value="${item.max}" min="0" data-id="${item.id}" data-field="max"
          style="width:52px;padding:0.2rem 0.4rem;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:0.35rem;font-size:0.78rem;text-align:center;color:var(--text-secondary);outline:none;background:var(--bg-surface);"/>
        <span style="font-size:0.72rem;color:var(--text-muted);">hrs</span>
        <button data-id="${item.id}" data-action="remove" style="background:none;border:none;cursor:pointer;color:var(--text-muted);font-size:1.1rem;padding:0;line-height:1;flex-shrink:0;">×</button>
      `;
      list.appendChild(row);
    });

    list.querySelectorAll('input[type="checkbox"]').forEach(el => {
      el.addEventListener('change', (e) => {
        const id = parseInt((e.target as HTMLInputElement).dataset.id!);
        const i  = items.find(x => x.id === id)!;
        i.enabled = (e.target as HTMLInputElement).checked;
        updateTotals();
      });
    });

    list.querySelectorAll('input[type="text"], input[type="number"]').forEach(el => {
      el.addEventListener('input', (e) => {
        const inp   = e.target as HTMLInputElement;
        const id    = parseInt(inp.dataset.id!);
        const field = inp.dataset.field!;
        const item  = items.find(x => x.id === id)!;
        if (field === 'label') item.label = inp.value;
        else (item as any)[field] = parseInt(inp.value) || 0;
        updateTotals();
      });
    });

    list.querySelectorAll('[data-action="remove"]').forEach(el => {
      el.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
        const id = parseInt((e.currentTarget as HTMLElement).dataset.id!);
        items = items.filter(x => x.id !== id);
        renderList(); updateTotals();
      });
    });
  }

  document.getElementById('add-item')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
    items.push({ id: nextId++, label: 'New scope item', min: 4, max: 8, enabled: true });
    renderList(); updateTotals();
  });

  document.getElementById('hourly-rate')?.addEventListener('input', updateTotals);

  renderList();
  updateTotals();
</script>
